{"title":"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Which of the following functions is continuous but not differentiable at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ea5b6f6de584d56d19a89cc4923a9d9a5cfa41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/0/3a0c8c9f662a34bc41a2a41153c9444c4b573c86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\dfrac{x}{x^{2}+1}  \\quad \t\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\dfrac{1}{x}  \\quad \t\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}  \\quad \t\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\ln (x^{2} + 1)  \\quad \t\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\dfrac{|x|}{x}  \\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"455\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can immediately eliminate answer choices B and E because they are not defined at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> due to having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the denominator, which is required for the function to be continuous at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Additionally, we know that the rest of the answer choices must be continuous because the limits from both sides are equal.\n</p><p>We can then look through each of the answer choices to see which ones are <i>not</i> differentiable:\n</p><p>For answer choice A, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/d/ecd27779034eeea3e9026ca0621db6330ad6c4ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y&#39; = \\frac{(x^{2} + 1) \\times \\frac{d}{dx}(x) - x \\times \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + 1)}{(x^{2} + 1)^{2}} = \\frac{x^{2} + 1 - x \\times 2x}{(x^{2} + 1)^{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"461\" height=\"47\" />. Plugging in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/c/01c618cf5efbb42e8eb7024a72c6a5a7efbea374.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0^{2} + 1 - 0 \\times 2 \\times 0}{(0^{2} + 1)^{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"148\" height=\"45\" />, which clearly simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Additionally, we can see that the limit from both sides of the derivatives equals to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This means that the function is differentiable, so we can eliminate it.\n</p><p>For answer choice C, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/1/2713d545a15a34a472ed02db8297a32e029facbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = \\sqrt[3]{x} = x^{\\frac{1}{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"23\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/8/ba89030408fa8c8f15f8c12fa6c8666ac1fbaff1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y&#39; = \\frac{1}{3} \\times x^{-\\frac{2}{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" />. However, this shows that the function is <i>not</i> differentiable at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/1/20173551b1b4fdaf26c74bbb577dc0846c38aa51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3} \\times x^{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"139\" height=\"43\" /> is <i>not</i> defined at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> due to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> resulting in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> being in the denominator of the fraction. This means that the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db134f5482128be1f3c1dfe9f775adb4288dea22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(C) } \\sqrt[3]{x}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"74\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest\">2025 TMTA Calculus</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 4 (page does not exist)\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_TMTA_High_School_Calculus_Contest_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 TMTA High School Calculus Contest Problems/Problem 4 (page does not exist)\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a cla